{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "id": "gr-ndesu",
  "connector": "〜んです",
  "gloss": "explaining: it’s that…",
  "title": "〜んです — explaining, and asking why",
  "pattern": "plain form + んです (casual: 〜んだ, 〜の); noun / な-adjective + なんです",
  "explanation": "んです adds a sense of explanation: there’s a reason or a story behind it. {頭|あたま}が{痛|いた}いんです — (the thing is) I have a headache. Asking with it shows interest: どうしたんですか — what’s wrong? In casual speech it becomes んだ, or の in questions: どうしたの？",
  "natural": "Explaining why you’re late, why you can’t come, what’s wrong: {電車|でんしゃ}が{遅|おく}れたんです.",
  "stiff": "Used too much, んです can sound pushy or emotional. For a plain fact with no story, like {学生|がくせい}です, leave it out.",
  "examples": [
    {
      "id": "gx-ndesu-1",
      "japanese": "すみません、頭が痛いんです。",
      "furigana": "すみません、{頭|あたま}が{痛|いた}いんです。",
      "english": "Sorry, I’ve got a headache.",
      "register": "polite",
